diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..02308cfed11055642c31aa85ad446df32f28e627
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,232 @@
+# Cyclic-code multiplier equivalence for arXiv:1002.2456
+
+| Profile | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | polynomial identity |
+| Certificate | exact symbolic group element: a root multiplier and its inverse coordinate multiplier |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: coefficient weights under a common scaling of all defining exponents |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed by Section III's prime-length multiplier criterion |
+
+## What the family is and whether to trust it
+
+Guenda's [*The Permutation Groups and the Equivalence of Cyclic and
+Quasi-Cyclic Codes*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.2456) studies which coordinate
+permutations can carry one cyclic code to another.  This family uses the exact
+prime-length setting from Section III.  A code `C(S)` of prime length `p` over
+`F_q` is specified by the exponents `S` of its defining zeros.  The instance
+packages `S` losslessly as the polynomial `D_S(z)=prod(z-s)` over `F_p` and
+gives two such polynomials.  The solver must return the multiplier `a` carrying
+the first root set to the second, together with `a^-1`, the corresponding
+coordinate multiplier.
+
+This is not an unsupported Track A claim.  Section III says that when
+`gcd(n,phi(n))=1`—hence for prime `n=p`—permutation equivalence is exactly
+equivalence by a multiplier.  The polynomial packaging is a lossless
+representation of the paper's defining set, but the search is carried by the
+paper-licensed multiplier reduction, so the profile deliberately says
+`licensed_reduction` rather than `native`.  Verification checks every
+coefficient of `D_T(z)=a^k D_S(z/a)` modulo `p`; it does not read the planted
+answer.  A separate demo audit reconstructed the actual cyclic-code generator
+matrices over `F_311` and confirmed that coordinate `i -> a^-1 i` makes their
+row spaces equal.
+
+All local correctness, density, attack, scaling, and canonicalization gates
+pass.  The required four-vendor oracle evidence is **not yet available**:
+OpenRouter rejected every bare, structural, and placebo request with HTTP 403
+`Key limit exceeded (total limit)`.  The harness correctly counted none of
+those errors as an attempt.  Accordingly this directory is reproducible and
+locally verified, but it should not be treated as a completed shipping hardness
+claim until the three oracle arms are rerun with quota.
+
+## Why it is Track B hard
+
+The paper does not prove average-case hardness.  Its Section III restriction
+instead supplies the mechanical algorithm: enumerate all `p-1` multipliers and
+test them.  The implementation uses the fastest scalar rejection available on
+this distribution, the constant coefficient, so the reference cost is
+`O(p log k+k)` modular operations.  At candidate shipping parameters
+`p=1,000,003`, `k=79`, eight runs tested 3,566,325 candidates and used
+35,664,006 counted exact operations in 9.659 seconds: 4,458,001 operations per
+instance on average.  It solved 8/8, as Track B requires.
+
+The compact route notices that the coefficient of `z^j` has weight `k-j`
+under root scaling.  The constant-term ratio is therefore `a^k`; because the
+generator chooses `gcd(k,p-1)=1`, one exponentiation by `k^-1 mod (p-1)`
+recovers the unique `a`.  This took at most 69 counted exact operations.  The
+benchmark tests finding that invariant, not the existence of an efficient
+algorithm, and the efficient algorithm is disclosed rather than hidden.
+
+The easy regimes deliberately avoided are Section II's four elementary codes
+(zero, whole space, repetition, and its dual) and Remark 2's length-`p` codes
+over `F_p`.  Here `7 <= |S| < p-1`, while the alphabet is `F_q` with prime
+`q>p` and `q=1 mod p`.  That congruence makes every `q`-cyclotomic class a
+singleton, so the sampled defining sets are valid.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `seed=7`, the complete demo statement is:
+
+```text
+CYCLIC-CODE EQUIVALENCE BY A COORDINATE MULTIPLIER
+
+Let p=31, a prime, and q=311, also prime.  Because q is congruent to
+1 modulo p, the field F_q contains an element zeta of multiplicative order p.
+For a subset S of the nonzero residues modulo p, define C(S) to be the cyclic
+linear code of length p over F_q whose defining zeros are exactly zeta^s for
+s in S.  The instance encodes S losslessly by its defining-exponent polynomial
+
+    D_S(z) = product over s in S of (z-s), computed in F_p[z].
+
+Both displayed polynomials are promised monic, squarefree, split completely
+over F_p, to have 7 distinct nonzero roots, and to have zero coefficient
+at z^6.  A missing exponent below means coefficient zero.  Polynomial
+term order has no meaning; coefficients are canonical residues 0 through p-1.
+
+Source code C(S), encoded by D_S:
+  exponent 7: coefficient 1
+  exponent 5: coefficient 25
+  exponent 4: coefficient 23
+  exponent 3: coefficient 3
+  exponent 2: coefficient 2
+  exponent 1: coefficient 1
+  exponent 0: coefficient 3
+
+Target code C(T), encoded by D_T:
+  exponent 7: coefficient 1
+  exponent 5: coefficient 9
+  exponent 4: coefficient 30
+  exponent 3: coefficient 30
+  exponent 2: coefficient 19
+  exponent 1: coefficient 16
+  exponent 0: coefficient 27
+
+Find integers a,b in the inclusive range 1 through 30 such that
+b is the multiplicative inverse of a modulo p and T={a*s mod p : s in S}.
+Equivalently, the exact coefficient identity is
+
+    D_T(z) = a^7 D_S(z/a) in F_p[z].
+
+With the coordinate convention used here, the permutation M_b sends coordinate
+i to b*i modulo p.  It carries C(S) to C(T), because a=b^(-1).  Exponents and
+coordinates are 0-based residues; the defining sets themselves exclude 0.
+The order of the two multipliers in the answer is fixed and may not be swapped.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as exactly one JSON object
+with the two shown keys and base-10 integer values, with no markdown inside the
+tags.  Example syntax (not necessarily a solution):
+<answer>{"root_multiplier":1,"coordinate_multiplier":1}</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The constructed answer is
+`<answer>{"root_multiplier":18,"coordinate_multiplier":19}</answer>` and
+verifies as `(True, "ok")`.  Replacing the inverse by 18 is rejected as
+`(False, "coordinate_multiplier is not the modular inverse of root_multiplier")`.
+A person can solve this demo by trying its 30 structurally valid multipliers;
+exact enumeration finds one answer.
+
+## Difficulty presets
