codex
Built a self-contained Track B generator based on the paper’s cyclic-code multiplier equivalence framework in Sections II–III of [arXiv:1002.2456](https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.2456).

Deliverables:

- [gen_1002_2456.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1002.2456/gen_1002_2456.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1002.2456/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1002.2456/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1002.2456/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1002.2456/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1002.2456/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local gates pass, including:

- 0/200,000 structure-aware random guesses.
- Seven attacks at 0/8 successes each.
- Reference algorithm solves 8/8, averaging 5,694 exact operations.
- Compact invariant route verifies 512/512 instances, maximum 265 operations.
- Canonical-key invariance passes 80/80 transformations.
- Determinism, parsing, JSON round-trip, and syntax checks pass.

The mandatory external hardening verdict remains blocked: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” on every redraw, so no valid oracle attempt occurred. The script-generated error transcripts were retained, and the README clearly marks the result as not yet shippable. I did not fabricate a verdict or incorrectly reject the family.
