 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# arXiv:1002.2456 — cyclic-code equivalence by multipliers
     2	
     3	This is a **Track B** generator. Its native domain is **algebra**, its object
     4	regime is **finite-field exact**, its computational core is **permutation**, and
     5	its certificate is an **integer tuple containing a multiplier and its inverse**.
     6	The solver is given cyclic codes through their native q-cyclotomic defining-set orbits, so
     7	`domain_essentiality="native"` and `reduction_kind="none"`. The intended
     8	intuition is an **invariant**: multiply the orbit representatives in the quotient
     9	group instead of matching and expanding possible orbit images one at a time.
    10	
    11	External status: all local gates pass, but this result is **not yet externally
    12	hardened or shippable**. The required bare, structural-hint, and placebo runs all
    13	reached OpenRouter and received HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” on every redraw.
    14	Those are service errors, not model failures; the script-produced transcripts are
    15	retained, and no oracle-hardness claim is made.
    16	
    17	## The problem
    18	
    19	The source is Kenza Guenda, [*The Permutation Groups and the Equivalence of
    20	Cyclic and Quasi-Cyclic Codes*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.2456). Section II
    21	defines permutation equivalence and its coordinate convention. Section III
    22	defines the multiplier `M_a: i -> a*i (mod p)`, Brand's set `H(P)`, and the
    23	q-cyclotomic structures used to restrict cyclic-code equivalences.
    24	
    25	An instance gives two length-p cyclic codes over `F_q`. Each code is represented
    26	exactly by one representative from each q-cyclotomic orbit in its defining zero
    27	set. The solver must return `[a,a_inverse]` for any coordinate multiplier mapping
    28	the first code to the second. Verification computes exact quotient-coset labels,
    29	checks their finite-set equality, and checks the inverse. Generation first samples
    30	`a`, then multiplies the first defining set by `a_inverse`; the answer is carried
    31	through that transformation and is never recovered by search.
    32	
    33	## Why Track B
    34	
    35	This paper does not license a Track A distributional-hardness claim. The paragraph
    36	before Lemma 10 states regimes in which equivalence reduces to a multiplier, and
    37	Theorem 15 explicitly identifies `H(P)` in small-Sylow regimes. Section II also
    38	notes the very easy case `ord_p(q)=p-1`, where only four elementary cyclic codes
    39	exist. This generator avoids that lookup regime: `ord_p(q)=m` ranges from 4 to
    40	256, still tiny relative to `p-1`, and the defining set is a proper nontrivial
    41	union of full orbits.
    42	
    43	The disclosed reference algorithm labels every `<q>`-coset by `x^m`, fixes one
    44	orbit of CODE 2, and tries its `t` possible image orbits in CODE 1. It costs
    45	`O(t*log(m) + t^2)`, solved 8/8 shipping instances, averaged 5,693.875
    46	counted exact operations, and averaged about 0.0005 seconds locally. Thus Track A
    47	would be false. The compact route works in `F_p^*/<q>`: if `P1` and `P2` are products of the two
    48	representative lists, then `P1/P2 = a^t` modulo `<q>`. Construction enforces
    49	`gcd(t,(p-1)/m)=1`, so one exponentiation yields some valid member of `a<q>`.
    50	The post-insight route used at most 265 exact operations over 512 shipping
    51	seeds—short enough for the stated cap, but unlike the reference scan it first
    52	requires seeing the quotient invariant.
    53	
    54	## Worked demo
    55	
    56	For `seed=0`, the complete rendered demo instance is:
    57	
    58	```text
    59	Cyclic-code equivalence by a coordinate multiplier
    60	
    61	All arithmetic in exponents is modulo the prime code length p=41.
    62	The code alphabet is the prime field F_q with q=173.  The residue of q modulo
    63	p is 9 and has exact multiplicative order m=4.
    64	
    65	For a nonzero residue r modulo p, define its q-cyclotomic orbit
    66	    O(r) = {r*q^j mod p : j=0,1,...,3}.
    67	Every orbit here has exactly 4 distinct elements.  A list R of representatives
    68	defines the exponent set D(R)=the union of O(r) over r in R.  Relative to a
    69	primitive p-th root alpha in F_(q^4), D(R) defines the length-p cyclic code
    70	C(R) over F_q whose code polynomials vanish at alpha^d for every d in D(R).
    71	The listed representatives have pairwise disjoint orbits; their printed order
    72	has no meaning, and replacing a representative by another element of its orbit
    73	does not change the code.
    74	
    75	CODE 1 has these 3 q-orbit representatives:
    76	[19, 33, 9]
    77	
    78	CODE 2 has these 3 q-orbit representatives:
    79	[36, 32, 13]
    80	
    81	For 1 <= a < p, the coordinate multiplier M_a sends coordinate i to a*i mod p
    82	(coordinates are 0-based, including coordinate 0).  With the convention above,
    83	M_a maps CODE 1 to CODE 2 exactly when multiplying every defining exponent of
    84	CODE 2 by a gives the defining exponent set of CODE 1.
    85	
    86	Find any such a and also give b=a^(-1) mod p.  Output exactly two canonical
    87	integers [a,b], each in the inclusive range 1,...,p-1.  The first entry is the
    88	forward multiplier from CODE 1 to CODE 2 under the stated convention, and the
    89	second is its inverse.
    90	Repetitions are not allowed for this instance.
    91	
    92	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as compact JSON [a,b].
    93	Example of the required syntax: <answer>[2,21]</answer>
    94	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    95	```
    96	
    97	The planted answer is `[10,37]`; `verify(inst,[10,37])` returns `(True,"ok")`.
    98	The quotient-product route independently produces `[33,5]`, which also verifies:
    99	there are exactly four valid multipliers, one coset of `<9>`. Swapping the planted
   100	fields gives `verify(inst,[37,10]) == (False,"forward multiplier does not map CODE
   101	2's defining set to CODE 1's")`. A person can solve this demo on paper: products
   102	are taken modulo 41 and the relevant quotient has order 10.
   103	
   104	## Difficulty presets
   105	
   106	| preset | requested `n` | actual `p` (seed 0) | orbit order `m` | orbits `t` | valid / candidate multipliers |
   107	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
   108	| demo | 41 | 41 | 4 | 3 | 4 / 38 |
   109	| easy | 20,000,000 | 20,000,033 | 16 | 31 | 16 / 20,000,030 |
   110	| medium | 80,000,000 | 80,000,513 | 64 | 53 | 64 / 80,000,510 |
   111	| hard (**configured shipping**) | 300,000,000 | 300,003,841 | 256 | 89 | 256 / 300,003,838 |
   112	
   113	No preset has an oracle verdict because the pool was unreachable. `escalate()`
   114	doubles both `n` and the orbit order, increasing the multiplier space, the
   115	integer bit cost, and the quotient-labelling work while keeping the two-residue
   116	answer and 89 displayed representatives fixed.
   117	
   118	## Gate results
   119	
   120	| gate | result | measurement |
   121	|---|---|---|
   122	| G1 | pass | 16/16 planted witnesses and 16/16 JSON round trips |
   123	| G2 | pass | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-range rejected with 5 reasons |
   124	| G3 | pass | prose/fenced response round-trips; garbage returns `None` |
   125	| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structured guesses; exact density 256/300,003,838 |
   126	| G5 | pass | reference scan 8/8; 45,551 total operations; demo brute-force count 4 |
   127	| G6 | pass | seven attacks all 0/8; reference algorithm separately succeeds 8/8 |
   128	| G7 | pass | doubled `n` builds at p=600,000,001 and keeps a two-residue answer |
   129	| G8 | pass | 80 invariant-key and 80 carried-witness checks; 20/20 distinct seeds |
   130	| G9(c) | pass | 21 chars, about 6 tokens, 2 atoms; max 265 route operations over 512 seeds |
   131	
   132	The failing Track B attacks are first/minimum/maximum representative ratios,
   133	ordinary-sum ratio, raw-product ratio, sorted-position vote, and 256 uniform
   134	random restarts. The successful quotient-label fixed-orbit scan is correctly
   135	disclosed only as `reference_algorithm`.
   136	
   137	## Oracle loop
   138	
   139	| preset | model | seed | result | reason |
   140	|---|---|---:|---|---|
   141	| easy | openai/gpt-5.6-terra | 964896830 | error | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded |
   142	| easy | openai/gpt-5.6-terra | 1395765912 | error | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded |
   143	| easy | x-ai/grok-4.6 | 1436776372 | error | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded |
   144	| easy | openai/gpt-5.6-terra | 983981921 | error | HTTP 403 key total limit exceeded |
   145	
   146	The harness correctly stopped without a verdict after four redraw failures.
   147	
   148	## G9 arms
   149	
   150	| arm | solved / valid attempts | harness errors | conclusion |
   151	|---|---:|---:|---|
   152	| bare | 0 / 0 | 4 | unmeasured |
   153	| structural hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unmeasured |
   154	| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unmeasured |
   155	
   156	`hinted - placebo` is undefined; the schema stores `0.0` only as a placeholder.
   157	The hint names the quotient-product invariant without giving an algorithmic chain.
   158	No claim about hint responsiveness is possible until valid oracle calls run.
   159	
   160	## Use
   161	
   162	```python
   163	import gen_1002_2456 as g
   164	
   165	inst = g.make_instance(seed=17, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
   166	statement = g.render(inst)
   167	candidate = g.parse_answer(
   168	    "<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>"
   169	)
   170	assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
   171	```
   172	
   173	After OpenRouter capacity is restored, rerun
   174	`python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_1002_2456.py` here, then rerun the hinted and
   175	placebo copies in separate directories. From the repository root, emission is
   176	`bash scripts/emit.sh 1002.2456` after a genuine `hardened` verdict exists.
   177	
   178	## Caveats
   179	
   180	- This is deliberately not a complexity-theoretic hardness claim. Exact software
   181	  solves the hard preset in well under a second; the benchmark tests
   182	  recognition and execution of a no-tool compression.
   183	- The exact density uses the proof that a t-element subset of the quotient cannot
   184	  have a nontrivial translation stabilizer when `gcd(t,(p-1)/m)=1`. The sampled
   185	  0/200,000 figure measures a uniform nonzero, non-self-inverse multiplier with
   186	  its inverse already filled in; it does not measure resistance to algebraic analysis.
   187	- Generation conditions on the listed cheap signatures failing. This prevents a
   188	  known planting leak but means the instance distribution is not an unconditional
   189	  random distribution of orbit representatives.
   190	- The quotient-product invariant itself solves every generated instance. That is
   191	  the intended Track B route, not an omitted attack. No support-splitting package,
   192	  general code-equivalence canonicalizer, or CAS was run; each would be expected
   193	  to succeed and would not strengthen this no-tool claim.
   194	- The quasi-cyclic conjectural results in Section IV are not used. This family uses
   195	  only the exact cyclic-code definitions and multiplier action from Sections II–III.
   196	- Prime testing is deterministic over the 64-bit range containing all presets and
   197	  tested escalations. Extremely large user-supplied `n` outside that range is not
   198	  supported by the stated primality guarantee.
   199	- Most importantly, the four-vendor oracle and both G9 comparison arms remain
   200	  unmeasured because the configured OpenRouter key has no remaining limit.

